## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a simple data structure with guaranteed O(1) operations and handle edge cases like duplicates and emptiness.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack technique to keep current minimum per depth
- Constant-time top and getMin
- Correct empty-state handling and error signaling

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.mins = [];
  }
  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    if (this.mins.length === 0) {
      this.mins.push(x);
    } else {
      this.mins.push(Math.min(x, this.mins[this.mins.length - 1]));
    }
  }
  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    this.stack.pop();
    this.mins.pop();
  }
  top() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }
  getMin() {
    if (this.mins.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.mins[this.mins.length - 1];
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to also support getMax() efficiently? 
- How would you optimize memory if input values are highly repetitive?

